**DEFINITION.** A (restricted) *equivalence relation* on $\mathbf{P}\omega$ is a symmetric and transitive relation on $\mathbf{P}\omega$.

Such relations are restricted because they are only reflexive on their domains—which are the same as their ranges—and these are the subsets with which the relations are concerned. We shall write $x \, A \, y$ for $\langle x, y \rangle \in A$ and $x : A$ for $x \, A \, x$. What we assume about these relations is the following:

$$(7.2) \quad x \, A \, y \text{ implies } y \, A \, x.$$

$$(7.3) \quad x \, A \, y \text{ and } y \, A \, z \text{ imply } x \, A \, z.$$

In case $a$ is a retract, we introduce an equivalence relation to correspond:

$$(7.4) \quad E_a = \{\langle x, x \rangle \mid x : a\}.$$

This is the identity relation restricted to the range of $a$. Such relations (for obvious reasons) and many others satisfy an additional *intersection property*:

$$(7.5) \quad x \, A \, y \text{ and } x \, A \, z \text{ imply } x \, A \, (y \cap z).$$

We shall not generally assume (7.5) in this short discussion, but it is often convenient.

Each equivalence relation represents a *space*: the space of all its equivalence classes. Such spaces form a category more extensive than the category of retracts studied above. The familiar functors can be extended to this larger category by these definitions:

$$(7.6) \quad A \to B = \{\langle \lambda x.\, u(x), \lambda x.\, v(x) \rangle \mid u(x) \, B \, v(y) \text{ whenever } x \, A \, y\},$$

$$(7.7) \quad A \times B = \{\langle \langle x, x' \rangle, \langle y, y' \rangle \rangle \mid x \, A \, y \text{ and } x' \, B \, y'\},$$

$$(7.8) \quad A + B = \{\langle \langle 0, x \rangle, \langle 0, y \rangle \rangle \mid x \, A \, y\} \cup \{\langle \langle 1, x' \rangle, \langle 1, y' \rangle \rangle \mid x' \, B \, y'\}.$$

<!-- page 42 -->

<!-- formalization -->

**Formalization.** Restricted equivalences are packaged as a structure with a binary relation that is symmetric and transitive. Membership $x:A$ is reflexivity $x\,A\,x$. The retract identity $E_a$, the functors (7.6)–(7.8), and the empty and singleton equivalences are the constructions below; symmetry and transitivity of each are part of the definition.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv`).** A restricted equivalence is a relation $A$ on $\mathbf{P}\omega$ satisfying (7.2) and (7.3).

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.mem`).** $x:A$ means $x\,A\,x$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.Ea`).** $E_a$ relates $x$ to $y$ just when $x:a$, $y:a$, and $x=y$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.arrowE`).** $A\to B$ relates $f$ to $g$ when both are graphs of their own application and $x\,A\,y$ implies $f(x)\,B\,g(y)$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.prodE`).** $A\times B$ relates $u$ to $v$ when both are pairs of their projections and the projections are related in $A$ and in $B$ respectively.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.sumE`).** $A+B$ relates $u$ to $v$ when both are left injections whose payloads are related in $A$, or both are right injections whose payloads are related in $B$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.emptyE`).** The empty restricted equivalence relates nothing.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.singletonE`).** The singleton on $a$ relates $x$ to $y$ just when $x=y=a$.

**Theorem (`singletonE.mem`).** $x$ is a member of the singleton on $a$ if and only if $x=a$.

*Proof.* Unfold membership as $x\,A\,x$. ∎

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.Nk`).** $N_k$ relates $x$ to $y$ when $x=y=\overline{n}$ for some $n<k$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.Nrel`).** $N$ relates $x$ to $y$ when $x=y=\overline{n}$ for some $n\in\omega$.

**Definition (`RestrictedEquiv.kerE`).** The kernel of $a$ relates $x$ to $y$ when $a(x)=a(y)$.

**Theorem (`kerE.repr`).** If $a$ is a retract, then $x$ is kernel-related to $a(x)$.

*Proof.* Retract-fixedness gives $a(a(x))=a(x)$. ∎

**Definition (`iterateE`).** The iterate equivalence at $f$ and $x$ relates $u$ to $v$ when $u=v=Z(n,f,x)$ for some $n$.
